# compose.service.yaml.schema
#
# Companion document for `compose.service.yaml`, the deployment fragment.
#
# ## The line that makes it a host
#
# `entrypoint: ["/usr/local/bin/agent-host-boot"]`. The image keeps the base's
# entrypoint on purpose, so the multiplexer is selected by the deployment rather
# than baked into the image. Two consequences, both intended:
#
# - the same image started without this override runs exactly one agent in one
#   workspace — the base's behaviour, unchanged, which is what an operator gets
#   from `docker run <image>` and what keeps this image composable with the rest
#   of the set;
# - the multiplexer is a run-time choice, so a deployment that wants a single
#   agent does not have to build a second image.
#
# ## Posture
#
# | Setting | Value | Why |
# |---------|-------|-----|
# | `user` | the base's account | Never root. The image already carries it; the fragment names it so a deployment that renames the account in one place stays consistent. |
# | `tty`, `stdin_open` | true | The container's process is a herdr client. |
# | `restart` | `unless-stopped` | The host comes back after a host restart; agents' sessions resume from the mounted tree. |
# | `ports` | absent | The SSH daemon listens on the loopback address inside the container and no port is published, so nothing outside can reach it. Opening it is two deliberate edits, not one: uncomment the `ports` block **and** set `AGENT_SSH_LISTEN`, shown commented. A published port alone forwards to an address nothing is listening on, and the client is refused with nothing to explain it. |
# | docker access | none | No socket mount. Docker CLI access goes through the pinned restrict-docker-api-access endpoint, shown commented. |
#
# ## Volumes
#
# `AGENT_HOST_STATE` is mounted at `AGENT_TREE`. One directory holds every
# agent's workspace and home, its supervision state below `.state`, and the SSH
# material below `.sshd`.
#
# The SSH material lives there by default — `AGENT_TREE/.sshd` is the boot
# program's default for `AGENT_SSHD_DIR` — and that default is the whole reason
# the host identity is stable: host keys generated at first boot are written
# inside the bind mount, so a recreate finds the same key and a client's
# `known_hosts` entry stays valid. Point `AGENT_SSHD_DIR` at `$HOME` and the key
# is regenerated with the container's layer on every recreate, and every client
# is asked to trust a new host.
#
# ## Validation
#
# ```
# AGENT_IDS=a,b AGENT_HOST_STATE=/srv/agents docker compose -f compose.service.yaml up -d
# docker inspect <container> --format '{{range .Mounts}}{{.Source}} -> {{.Destination}}{{println}}{{end}}'
# docker inspect <container> --format '{{json .NetworkSettings.Ports}}'   # no published port
# ```
#
# Opening remote attach needs both edits, and the pair is what the acceptance
# script checks for in the shipped fragment:
#
# ```
# # ports:
# #   - "0.0.0.0:2222:2222"
# environment:
#   AGENT_SSH_LISTEN: "0.0.0.0"        # without this the published port reaches nothing
# ```
